{
  "image_name": "fa53c43c67c2b5b51f60016d6518d77e.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes defect-assisted electronic transitions in a semiconductor and how they appear in configuration-coordinate diagrams. On the left, the band structure is shown with a conduction band at the top and a valence band at the bottom. Between them are localized defect states: shallow donors near the conduction band, shallow acceptors near the valence band, and a deeper donor/acceptor level that acts as a recombination centre. Electrons and holes can be weakly bound to the shallow states, while carriers from the bands can be captured by the deep centre, where recombination occurs. The arrows indicate carrier capture and release pathways linking the bands and defect levels.\n\nThe two plots on the right illustrate the optical signatures of such defects using parabolic potential-energy curves for two charge states. Energy is plotted against configuration coordinate, so vertical arrows represent optical absorption without immediate lattice relaxation, while the downward separation marks the emission energy. The “ZPL” arrow indicates the zero-phonon line, the purely electronic transition energy. Comparing the two diagrams, one case shows stronger overlap between the two potential wells and a smaller lattice shift, while the other shows a larger displacement, implying stronger lattice relaxation and a larger difference between absorption and emission.",
